.TH XPAR 1 "2026-08-26" "xpar 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xpar \- error and erasure correction for guarding data integrity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xpar
.I verb
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B xpar
.I set
.RS
same as
.B xpar verify
.IR set .
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xpar
creates recovery volumes for files and directories. These volumes can restore
data damaged by bad sectors, bit flips, truncation, media rot, or transmission
failures.
.SH VERBS
Any unambiguous prefix of a verb is accepted;  an exact match always wins
over a prefix match.
.TP
.B create
Build a redundancy set from the input paths. It refuses a directory
argument without
.BR \-R ,
an output base ending in a
.BI .g NNN
generation suffix, a
.B \-\-cell
larger than the slice, a
.B \-\-burst
or
.B \-\-depth
the memory budget cannot honour, and
.B \-\-labels
outside
.BR \-\-layout=split .
Re-run over a tree it already protects, it skips its own volumes and
says which.
.TP
.B verify
Checks the integrity of the data and writes nothing beside the set. An
armoured archive is staged in memory when its plaintext fits
.BR \-m ,
otherwise under
.BR TMPDIR ,
.B TMP
or
.BR TEMP ,
and the stage is removed on exit; only when none of those is set and the
plaintext exceeds
.B \-m
does the stage fall back beside the archive. The exit codes distinguish
clean, damaged but repairable, and hopelessly damaged data.
.TP
.B repair
Verify and repair errors or erasures.
.B \-\-in\-place
also recreates missing empty files, directories and symbolic links from
the manifest, restoring their recorded mode and times; rewrites a
packet-bearing volume whose packets fail their checksums from intact
replicas in the other volumes; and restores a split data volume found
under another name or at the wrong length to its recorded name and
length.
.TP
.B scrub
Read all data and recovery volumes and report corrected symbols per codeword.
With
.B \-\-deep
recompute recovery data from the protected data.
.TP
.B extract
Reconstruct data from a split or armoured set (see
.BR \-\-layout ).
A sidecar set protects files already on disk and needs no extraction. On
an armoured set it corrects inner-code damage as it reads, and exits 2,
never 4, when the data cannot be reproduced.
.TP
.B recover
Regenerate one volume of a set from the others.
.TP
.B addrecovery
Add recovery slices to an existing generation.
.TP
.B add
Append a generation protecting new or changed files.
.TP
.B consolidate
Collapse a chain of generations into one through re-encoding. Without
.B \-r
it inherits the widest redundancy any generation of the chain carries,
not the newest generation's, which may store nothing at all.
.TP
.B prune
Remove older generations from a chain, keeping any generation that a
surviving entry still depends on unless
.B \-f
is given.
.PP
Both refuse, with exit status 2, while any generation of the chain is
unreadable: a member whose volumes are on disk but whose descriptor
survives neither in them nor in the replicas its own recovery volumes
carry is reported as damaged, never dropped, since rewriting the chain
around it would destroy what it protects.
.TP
.B list
Print the manifest of the set.
.TP
.B info
Print geometry, codec, plan and redundancy, together with the creator
string, any comment packets, and, for
.BR \-\-layout=armoured ,
the armour overhead the archive carries on disk rather than the code's
nominal rate.
.B \-\-deps
adds the per-generation dependency table that
.B prune
consults; its
.B superseded/R
column counts the slices of a generation that nothing still reads against
the recovery that generation carries.
.TP
.B explain
Print the hand-recovery recipe: the exact
.BR dd (1)
commands that recover this particular set's armoured region without xpar
(without error or erasure correction). That region is the protected data
only under
.BR \-\-layout=armoured ;
in a sidecar or split set the data is the original files, and the recipe
extracts the critical metadata group instead.
.TP
.B undo
Replay an in-place repair journal after a crash interrupted an operation.
.TP
.B recover\-prologue
Recover the parameters of a destroyed armoured prologue via brute-force.
A frame the inner code can still correct counts as a decode, so damage
short of the code's capacity does not hide a framing.
.TP
.B benchmark
Measure xpar's low-level SIMD kernels.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options must follow the verb.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Repeatable.
.B \-vv
reports per-slice detail.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Diagnostics only.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite, and permit the guarded operations.
.TP
.BI \-j " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-jobs= N
Worker threads. Default is one per core.
.TP
.BI \-m " SIZE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-memory= SIZE
Working-set limit for the planner. When no plan fits, xpar refuses and
names the three numbers that would make one fit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write JSON Lines to the standard output; the human-readable report goes to
the standard error instead.
.TP
.BR \-\-progress ", " \-\-no\-progress
Report progress on the standard error, at most one line a second, or never
report it. Without either, progress is shown only on a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-color= WHEN
.IR auto " (default), " always ", " never .
.TP
.B \-\-reproducible
Omit the source timestamps and the version suffix of the creator string,
ensuring reproducibility across machines of SHA-exact sets. No host name is
recorded in any case. Permissions, attributes and links describe the data
rather than the machine, so they are still recorded; name a class with
.B \-\-preserve
to keep one this would otherwise drop. Setting
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
in the environment turns this on, which is what a reproducible build
expects, so a set written under one records no timestamps.
.TP
.BI \-\-simd= TIER
Force a SIMD tier.
.I auto
is the default. A tier the hardware cannot run is refused rather than
silently downgraded.
.TP
.BI \-\-auth\-key= FILE
Read the authentication key from
.IR FILE .
An empty file is rejected. Authentication does not encrypt the set. Every
verb that opens an authenticated set needs the key and exits 6 without it;
see
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Sizes take
.BR K ", " M ", " G ", " T
(1024-based) or
.BR KB ", " MB ", " GB ", " TB
(1000-based); an unsuffixed number is bytes.
.SH CREATE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " BASE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-output= BASE
Base name for output volumes.
.TP
.BI \-r " SPEC" "\fR, \fP" \-\-recovery= SPEC
Recovery count, percentage, size, or multiple; see
.BR REDUNDANCY .
Omitting it makes
.B create
use five per cent, while
.B add
and
.B consolidate
inherit the ratio the generation being extended already carries, and say
so; they warn when the result still protects less than the set did.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-recovery= N
Minimum derived recovery-slice count.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-recovery= SPEC
Recovery-axis space reserved for later top-ups. The axis is widened to
hold it whatever the codec, so
.B addrecovery
can later grow to that ceiling.
.TP
.BI \-s " SIZE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-slice\-size= SIZE
Set slice size. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI \-b " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-slices= N
Plan for this many data slices. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BI \-\-cell= SIZE
Set the cell size Y, which is the erasure unit: a fault costs the cell
that holds it rather than the whole slice, and the recovery budget is
spent per column of cells, not per set. A multiple of 64 bytes and at
least 4096. Smaller cells localise damage more finely and cost more
checksum metadata. Without it the planner takes min(64 KiB, Z), so a
slice smaller than 64 KiB has exactly one cell and a slice under 4096
bytes has none at all, the erasure unit then being the whole slice. An
explicit
.B \-\-cell
larger than the slice
.B create
planned is refused, not clamped.
.TP
.BI \-\-layout= WHICH
.IR sidecar " (default), " split ", or " armoured .
.B add
takes the layout of the chain it extends and refuses to change it, since a
split or armoured generation owns the bytes it protects and a chain that
mixed layouts could not be extracted as one set.
.B consolidate
writes a fresh generation zero, so it inherits the chain's layout but does
accept a different one.
.TP
.BI \-\-codec= WHICH
.IR auto " (default), " fft ", or " matrix .
.TP
.BI \-\-field= W
.IR auto " (default), 8, or 16-bit outer field."
.TP
.BI \-\-align= WHICH
.IR none " (default), " slice ", or " 1k .
.TP
.BI \-\-slice\-tag= W
.IR none ", 8 (default), or 16 bytes."
.TP
.BI \-\-armour= WHICH
.IR none ", " metadata ", or " all .
.I metadata
is the default for
.B \-\-layout=sidecar
and
.BR \-\-layout=split ,
and covers the critical metadata group alone.
.I all
additionally wraps every SLTG and SLCL slice-table packet and every RCVS
recovery slice in an inner code of its own, so bit rot in a recovery
volume costs a few parity symbols rather than a whole slice. It costs
about 14 percent of the recovery volumes at the default GF(2^8)
RS(255,223) code, which is about 1.5 percent of the protected data at
.BR \-r " 10%."
Once a recovery slice is large enough for the wider GF(2^16) code to pay
for its 128 KiB frame, that code is chosen instead and the cost falls
under 2 percent;
.B \-\-armour\-field
forces either field. Reading an
.B all
set materialises each wrapped packet, so it needs about as much memory as
the recovery data it holds.
.I all
is the default and the only level for
.BR \-\-layout=armoured ,
which armours the whole archive. A bare split data volume carries no
packets and is never armoured.
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-field= W
.IR auto " (default), 8, or 16-bit inner field."
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-t= N
Correct up to
.I N
symbols per inner-code codeword. Use four or more.
.B \-\-armour\-t=1
leaves a codeword two parity symbols, which is enough to correct one
symbol and not enough to tell two apart from one, so almost every
two-symbol error is silently miscorrected and only the outer checksums
notice. A
.I N
that would leave the codeword storing more than four times its plaintext
is refused rather than clamped, and the refusal names the largest
.I N
the input affords.
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-pct= P
Set inner-code overhead relative to the protected data, so that 2t/k is
at most
.IR P .
Greater than zero and at most 50 percent. Excludes
.BR \-\-armour\-t .
.TP
.BI \-\-burst= SIZE
Choose interleave depth for this burst size. Excludes
.BR \-\-depth .
.TP
.BI \-\-depth= D
Set interleave depth; default 1. An interleave the working-set limit
cannot hold is refused rather than quietly reduced, whichever layout
carries the inner code, and the refusal names both the
.B \-m
the request would need and the largest
.B \-\-burst
or
.B \-\-depth
the current budget affords, computed the way the acceptance test computes
it. A
.B \-\-burst
is rounded up, never down, so the depth chosen delivers at least the
tolerance asked for.
.TP
.BI \-\-volumes= WHICH
.IR ladder " (default), " equal ", or a positive count."
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup= WHICH
.IR none ", " file " (default), or " chunk .
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-chunk= SIZE
Chunk size for chunk deduplication.
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-memory= SIZE
Memory ceiling for the chunk-fingerprint index, which only
.B \-\-dedup=chunk
builds; the other levels need no index and ignore it. Exceeding it is a
refusal, not a silent downgrade.
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-max\-refs= N
Maximum aliases of one stored range. Zero, the default, does not limit
them.
.TP
.BI \-\-preserve= LIST
Metadata to record. Tokens are
.BR mtime , atime , ctime , btime , times , mode , setid , attrs , owner ,
.BR xattr , xattr-all , links , all , none .
A bare list replaces the default; prefixes
.B +
and
.B \-
add and remove tokens. The default is
.BR mtime,mode,attrs,links .
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recurse
Descend into directories.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude= GLOB
Exclude matching paths; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-include= GLOB
Repeatable. Supplying it at all restricts the selection to matching
paths; it does not re-admit paths that
.B \-\-exclude
removed.
.TP
.B \-\-follow\-symlinks
Store target content rather than the symbolic link.
.TP
.BI \-\-base= DIR
Store names relative to
.IR DIR ,
which must be the input itself or a directory containing it; anything
else is a usage error rather than a silent fallback. Relative and
absolute spellings meet, so
.I DIR
may be absolute while the inputs are not. Naming the input directory
itself stores its children under their own names.
.TP
.B \-\-labels
Write a label file per volume. Only
.B \-\-layout=split
has data volumes to label, so the other layouts refuse it.
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-only
Omit public CRC and whole-file hashes from an authenticated set. It works
at any set size.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify\-after
Skip the post-write read-back pass. It never defers a check of what may
be stored: a manifest the readers would refuse, such as one where two
inputs share a stored name, is settled before the first byte is written
and costs exit status 4.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Refuse a name the format cannot carry rather than skipping it. Without
it such a name is reported and skipped, as an unreadable special file is,
and the rest of the tree is still protected.
.TP
.B \-\-spool
Stage a pipe in a temporary file beside the output.
.TP
.BI \-\-spool\-dir= DIR
Stage it under
.I DIR
instead, which implies
.BR \-\-spool .
.TP
.BI \-\-stdin\-name= PATH
Manifest path for a lone standard-input argument.
.PP
.B add
and
.B consolidate
also accept the create options.
.SH OTHER VERB OPTIONS
.TP
.BR "verify \-\-fast"
Check tags without the algebraic pass.
.TP
.BR "verify \-\-strong"
Check the BLAKE3 slice tags, not just the CRC32C slice checksums.
Entry hashes are checked whenever
.B \-\-fast
is absent, with or without this.
.B verify
warns when recovery slices themselves fail their checksum, since the set
can then be clean and still be protected by fewer slices than it names.
It warns in the same way about slices a later generation superseded:
those count as erasures for the decoder, so they can exhaust an older
generation's recovery, but with nothing damaged there is nothing to
decode and the verdict stays clean. They lower the verdict only once
real damage has to be localised against them.
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync=" WHICH ", " "repair \-\-resync=" WHICH
.IR off ", " auto " (default), or " always .
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync\-step=" N ", " "repair \-\-resync\-step=" N
Sample every
.I Nth
candidate offset.
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync\-window=" SIZE ", " "repair \-\-resync\-window=" SIZE
Search this far on either side of an expected offset.
.TP
.BR "verify \-\-resync\-exhaustive" ", " "repair \-\-resync\-exhaustive"
Confirm every misplaced-data candidate. Deleted bytes rarely leave one
dominant displacement, so recovering from a deletion generally needs
.B \-\-resync=always
together with this option; the diagnostic says so when it applies.
.TP
.BI \-\-scan= DIR
Also search
.I DIR
for this set's volumes, index and recovery alike, when they are not
beside the set. Every verb that opens a set takes it:
.BR verify ", " repair ", " scrub ", " extract ", " recover ", "
.BR addrecovery ", " add ", " consolidate ", " prune ", " list ", "
.BR info " and " explain .
A volume found beside the set wins; the scanned directory supplies only
what is missing. It never moves the set: the protected entries always
resolve beside the set named on the command line, so a scan directory
holding a stale copy of the data cannot be mistaken for it.
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-in\-place" ", " "repair \-\-to=DIR"
Rewrite originals, or write a repaired tree under
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-backup" ", " "repair \-\-paranoid"
Keep damaged originals, or re-read and re-verify every write.
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-keep\-journal" ", " "repair \-\-no\-journal"
Keep the journal after success, or disable it. These exclude each other.
The journal is created with mode 0600 and is never written through a
symbolic link planted under its name.
.TP
.B "repair \-\-replace\-journal"
Discard an undo journal an earlier repair left behind and write a new one.
Without it such a journal stops the repair, because replacing it would end
any chance of undoing the repair that wrote it. This is deliberately not
.BR \-f :
a set created with
.B \-\-slice\-tag=none
already requires
.B \-f
to be repaired in place, which is an unrelated decision.
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-dry\-run" ", " "repair \-\-exit\-on\-change"
Report only, or exit 1 when anything was repaired. The two combine: a dry
run with
.B \-\-exit\-on\-change
exits 1 when writes would be made and 0 when they would not.
.TP
.BR "scrub \-\-deep" ", " "scrub \-\-rewrite" ", " "scrub \-\-rebuild\-cells"
Re-encode the recovery data from the protected data and compare it,
write corrections back, or rebuild per-cell CRCs.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-to=" DIR
Extract below
.IR DIR .
.TP
.B "extract \-\-stdout"
Write the one entry of a single-entry set to standard output.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-preserve=" LIST
Apply the metadata classes described under create.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-owner\-map=" WHICH
.IR name " (default) or " numeric .
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-require=" LIST
Turn the named metadata degradations into errors.
.TP
.B "extract \-\-strict\-names"
Apply the Windows and DOS naming rules whatever the host is.
.TP
.BI "recover \-\-volume=" WHICH
Regenerate the named or numbered volume.
.TP
.BI "recover \-\-to=" DIR
Write the recovered volume below
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI "addrecovery \-r " SPEC
Add recovery slices within the reserved axis.
.TP
.BI "add \-\-rescan=" WHICH
.IR stat " (default), " hash ", or " none .
Under
.I hash
an entry whose bytes still hash to what the chain records inherits those
bytes and only its metadata is rewritten, so a touch or a chmod adds no
stream data.
.I stat
may re-store such an entry.
.TP
.BR "add \-\-verify\-unchanged" ", " "add \-\-allow\-missing"
Alias for
.BR \-\-rescan=hash ,
or continue past a vanished entry.
.TP
.BI "add \-\-dedup\-scope=" WHICH
.IR generation " (default) or " chain .
.TP
.BR "consolidate \-\-replace" ", " "consolidate \-\-dry\-run"
Remove collapsed generations, or report without writing.
.TP
.BI "prune \-\-before=" G
Remove every generation before
.IR G .
.TP
.BI "prune \-\-generation=" G
Remove generation
.IR G ;
repeatable.
.TP
.B "prune \-\-dry\-run"
Report without modifying the chain.
.TP
.BR "list \-\-links" ", " "list \-\-dedup"
Group link aliases, or show extents and reference counts.
.TP
.B "info \-\-deps"
Show per-generation dependencies used by
.BR prune .
.TP
.B "benchmark \-\-tiers"
List and time every runnable kernel tier after differential tests.
.PP
.B \-\-generation=G
selects by generation number or set-ID prefix where accepted.
.B \-\-chain
processes every generation oldest first where accepted.
Without
.BR \-\-generation ,
.BR verify ,
.B repair
and
.B scrub
already do that on a set of more than one generation, because a generation
can neither check nor rebuild the bytes it inherits from an ancestor;
.B \-\-chain
is then the default and naming it changes nothing. Damage in bytes an
ancestor owns is reported as
.RB \(lq N
entries damaged in data inherited from generation
.IR G \(rq,
and slices a later generation has superseded count as erasures against
the older generation's verdict, which says so and points at
.BR consolidate .
With
.B \-\-generation
they stay on the one generation and say so when damage falls in inherited
data.
.SH LAYOUTS
.TP
.B sidecar
Original files remain external. The index is
.IB base .xpa;
recovery volumes contain packets. Originals have outer-code protection but
are not inner-armoured; the inner code covers the critical metadata group,
and the slice tables and recovery slices as well under
.BR \-\-armour=all .
.TP
.B split
Bare
.IB base .d NN
volumes hold consecutive set-stream ranges with no header, trailer, or tail
padding. Concatenating them numerically yields the exact stream. Index and
recovery volumes contain packets, and as in the sidecar layout the inner
code covers the critical metadata group, extended by
.B \-\-armour=all
to the slice tables and the recovery slices. The bare data volumes stay
raw by definition.
.TP
.B armoured
One
.IB base .xpa
contains three protected prologue copies and an inner-coded packet stream
holding manifest, data, and recovery. Use
.B extract
rather than concatenation.
.PP
Names are not identities. Readers bind volumes and generations by set IDs,
parent IDs, layout entries, and bare-volume content tags.
.SH REDUNDANCY
.B \-r
accepts four forms:
.TP
.B 100
a slice count.
.TP
.B 15.7%
a percentage of the protected data.
.TP
.B 2.5M
an absolute number of recovery bytes.
.TP
.B 1x
a multiple of the data, so
.B \-r 1x
is mirroring.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; for
.BR verify ,
no damage.
.TP
.B 1
.BR verify :
damage found and repairable.
.BR "repair \-\-exit\-on\-change" :
something was repaired, or, under
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
would have been.
.TP
.B 2
Damage found and not repairable with the available recovery. This covers
every unrepairable data condition:
.B verify
and
.BR repair ,
an
.B extract
whose entries cannot be reproduced, a
.B recover
short of recovery slices, and a
.B recover\-prologue
that finds no framing.
.TP
.B 3
Set not found, not an xpar set, or an unsupported format version.
.TP
.B 4
Usage error, including a geometry or planner request that is
self-contradictory rather than merely too large: a
.B \-\-cell
above the slice size, an interleave beyond
.BR \-m ,
.B \-\-labels
outside the split layout, or an unusable output base.
.TP
.B 5
I/O error.
.TP
.B 6
Authentication failure: missing key, wrong key, or MAC mismatch.
.TP
.B 7
No feasible plan under
.BR \-m ,
or out of memory. Nothing else uses it: a request refused for its shape
rather than for the budget exits 4.
.TP
.B 8
Internal error. This is a bug.
.PP
.B repair
without
.B \-\-exit\-on\-change
exits 0 whether or not it had to do anything, which is what a cron job
wants.
.B verify
distinguishes 0, 1 and 2, which is what a monitoring check wants.
.SH EXAMPLES
Protect a file at 10% redundancy, then check it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar create \-r 10% \-o backup movie.mkv
xpar verify backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Repair in place after a bad sector, keeping an undo journal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar repair \-\-in\-place backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Protect a directory tree, deduplicating identical files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar create \-r 15% \-\-dedup=file \-o photos \-R ~/Pictures
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch a medium age. The histogram of corrected symbols per codeword is the
only signal that a disc is degrading before it fails:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar scrub photos.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the recipe that recovers the data without xpar:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar explain backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
If present, implies
.BR \-\-reproducible .
The value is not embedded in the set. Unless explicitly requested with
.BR \-\-preserve ,
source timestamps and ownership metadata are omitted and the creator
string contains no version. No host name is stored under any setting.
.TP
.BR TMPDIR ", " TMP ", " TEMP
The first of these that is set names the directory where
.B verify
stages the plaintext of an armoured archive too large for
.BR \-m .
With none set the stage is written beside the archive instead. It is
removed when the verb ends.
.SH SECURITY
Input volumes and manifests are by default considered untrusted.  xpar validates
packet and nested record lengths before allocation, the total allocation is also
further bounded by the input size, and continues past isolated invalid packets
to find replicated copies.
.PP
Extraction rejects empty and absolute names, drive and UNC names,
.B .
and
.B ..
components, control bytes, duplicate output paths, and invalid path
components (platform-specific). It does not follow an existing symlink
while constructing an output path. Hard-link targets must be regular
entries in the same effective manifest. Privileged mode bits,
ownership, extended attributes and links are applied only when
requested; unsupported restoration is reported, and
.B \-\-require
makes it fatal.
.PP
.B \-\-auth\-key
authenticates metadata and content but provides neither encryption nor
freshness.
An authenticated archive requires the key for every verb, including
.BR explain ;
a missing or wrong key exits 6 everywhere.
.PP
Authentication only protects a set when the verifier always supplies the
key. An attacker who does not have it cannot forge a MAC, but can strip
the AUTH packets and re-tag the whole set, and a keyless
.B verify
then reports that unauthenticated set clean. Nothing inside an archive can
oblige a reader to present a key, so the discipline has to live in the
script that calls xpar.
.PP
Public hashes retained without
.B \-\-auth\-only
remain useful after authentication, but their containing packet cannot be
trusted without its packet key.
.SH VERSION 1 ARCHIVES
Version 2.0 does not read version 1 archives and ships no version 1
decoder. A version 1 file handed to any verb is detected and refused with
a message naming the kind of version 1 object it is, and its version where
the format records one. To migrate, decode with xpar 1.x and re-protect
with this version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB base .xpa
The index volume: the manifest, the slice tables and the volume layout.
.TP
.IB base .v NN + MM .xpa
A recovery volume holding
.I MM
recovery slices, the first of which covers the stream from slice
.IR NN .
The number is a slice index, not a Reed-Solomon exponent.
Each number is padded to two digits, or to as many as the widest value in
that position needs.
.TP
.IB base .d NN
A bare split-layout data volume. Numeric order reconstructs the set stream.
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .xpa
The index volume for generation
.I GGG
after generation zero.
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .v NN + MM .xpa
A recovery volume owned by generation
.IR GGG .
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .d NN
A split data volume owned by generation
.IR GGG .
.TP
.IB base .d NN .xpa
Optional volume label written beside each bare data volume by
.BR \-\-labels .
.TP
.IB base .xparundo
An in-place repair journal. Its presence means a repair was interrupted;
.B xpar undo
replays it.
